%I A129288 #51 Nov 16 2026 17:40:18
%S A129288 0,36,39,123,319,336,820,1960,2059,4879,11523,12100,28536,67260,70623,
%T A129288 166419,392119,411720,970060,2285536,2399779,5654023,13321179,
%U A129288 13987036,32954160,77641620,81522519,192071019,452528623,475148160,1119472036,2637530200,2769366523
%N A129288 Nonnegative values x of solutions (x, y) to the Diophantine equation x^2 + (x + 41)^2 = y^2.
%C A129288 Also values x of Pythagorean triples (x, x+41, y).
%C A129288 Corresponding values y of solutions (x, y) are in A157257.
%C A129288 lim_{n -> infinity} a(n)/a(n-3) = 3 + 2*sqrt(2).
%C A129288 lim_{n -> infinity} a(n)/a(n-1) = (7 + 2*sqrt(2))/(7 - 2*sqrt(2)) for n mod 3 = {1, 2}.
%C A129288 lim_{n -> infinity} a(n)/a(n-1) = (3 + 2*sqrt(2))*(7 - 2*sqrt(2))^2/(7 + 2*sqrt(2))^2 for n mod 3 = 0.
%C A129288 For the generic case x^2 + (x + p)^2 = y^2 with p = m^2 - 8 a (prime) number in A028886, m >= 7 (p >= 41), the first three consecutive solutions are (0, p), (4*m+8, m^2+4*m+8), (3*m^2-20*m+32, 5*m^2-28*m+40), and the other solutions are defined by (X(n), Y(n)) = (3*X(n-3) + 2*Y(n-3) + p, 4*X(n-3) + 3*Y(n-3) + 2*p), X(n) = 6*X(n-3) - X(n-6) + 2*p, and Y(n) = 6*Y(n-3) - Y(n-6) (can be easily proved using X(n) = 3*X(n-3) + 2*Y(n-3) + p, and Y(n) = 4*X(n-3) + 3*Y(n-3) + 2*p). - _Mohamed Bouhamida_, Jun 03 2026
%C A129288 The nonnegative solutions of x^2 + (x+41)^2 = y^2 form exactly three Pell orbits with canonical seeds (x,y) = (0,41), (36,85), and (39,89). In the notation of the cited Pell-orbit catalog, H(41) = 3 and P(41) = 2: three solution streams are needed to obtain the increasing x-sequence, and two of these streams consist of primitive Pythagorean triples. - _Farhad Banazadeh_, Nov 09 2026
%H A129288 G. C. Greubel, <a href="/A129288/b129288.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a>
%H A129288 Farhad Banazadeh, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23592032">A Unified Pell-Orbit Theory for the Diophantine Equation x^2+(x+d)^2=y^2: Complete Parametrization, Recurrences, and an OEIS Catalogue, </a>, Zenodo (2026), Section 11.4.6.
%H A129288 <a href="/index/Rec#order_07">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (1,0,6,-6,0,-1,1).
%F A129288 a(n) = 6*a(n-3) - a(n-6) + 82 for n > 6; a(1)=0, a(2)=36, a(3)=39, a(4)=123, a(5)=319, a(6)=336.
%F A129288 G.f.: x*(36 + 3*x + 84*x^2 - 20*x^3 - x^4 - 20*x^5)/((1-x)*(1 - 6*x^3 + x^6)).
%F A129288 a(3*k + 1) = 41*A001652(k) for k >= 0.
%e A129288 For p=41 (m=7) the first three consecutive solutions are (0, 41), (36, 85), (39, 89).
%e A129288 The three canonical seeds give the triples (0,41,41), (36,77,85), and (39,80,89). Applying the Pell transformation (x,y) -> (3*x + 2*y + 41, 4*x + 3*y + 82) gives the next triples (123,164,205), (319,360,481), and (336,377,505), respectively. - _Farhad Banazadeh_, Nov 09 2026
%t A129288 LinearRecurrence[{1,0,6,-6,0,-1,1},{0,36,39,123,319,336,820},40] (* _Harvey P. Dale_, Jan 18 2015 *)
%o A129288 (PARI) forstep(n=0, 1200000000, [3, 1], if(issquare(2*n^2+82*n+1681), print1(n, ", ")))
%o A129288 (Magma) m:=25; R<x>:=PowerSeriesRing(Integers(), m); [0] cat Coefficients(R!(x*(36+3*x+84*x^2-20*x^3-x^4-20*x^5)/((1-x)*(1-6*x^3+ x^6)))); // _G. C. Greubel_, May 07 2018
%o A129288 (Python)
%o A129288 def a129288(n):
%o A129288     if n < 1:
%o A129288         raise ValueError("n must be >= 1")
%o A129288     streams = [(0, 41), (36, 85), (39, 89)]
%o A129288     for _ in range(n - 1):
%o A129288         i = min(range(3), key=lambda j: streams[j][0])
%o A129288         x, y = streams[i]
%o A129288         streams[i] = (3*x + 2*y + 41, 4*x + 3*y + 82)
%o A129288     return min(x for x, y in streams)
%o A129288 print([a129288(n) for n in range(1, 41)]) # _Farhad Banazadeh_, Nov 09 2026
%Y A129288 Cf. A157257, A001652, A156035 (decimal expansion of 3 + 2*sqrt(2)), A157258 (decimal expansion of 7 + 2*sqrt(2)), A157259 (decimal expansion of 7 - 2*sqrt(2)), A157260 (decimal expansion of (7 + 2*sqrt(2))/(7 - 2*sqrt(2))).
%Y A129288 Cf. A028886 (primes of the form m^2 - 8).
%Y A129288 Cf. A118674 (d=31), A118675 (d=47), A129836 (d=97) for related fixed-difference equations and Pell-orbit decompositions.
